Jim Miller wrote:
...
> This is also a key point.  The community has the collective resources,
> talent, and time to develop our game, if motivated to.  The current
> devteam serves at the community's pleasure.  I think we forget this
> sometimes.

Perhaps because that's just plain wrong?

The current DevTeam _own_ NetHack; we get new versions of it at their 
pleasure, not the other way around.  This isn't Debian, where (as I 
understand) ownership resides with the user community which has a 
formalized right to elect project managers and developers.

The license under which the DevTeam has distributed all versions of 
NetHack to date does mean that they cannot revoke our right to play what 
they've released, tweak its source and redistribute it, but it does 
_not_ give us any ownership or control of the "official" version or 
guarantee us any input in future releases.

> My inclination would be to continue to name the open project
> "NetHack", but give it a version number several steps higher, or start
> a different versioning system, to indicate the change in development;
> and also to clearly explain these changes in the game's 'V'ersion
> information.

While the NetHack license doesn't explicitly say that derivative works 
cannot be distributed under the name "NetHack", it does give several 
requirements for labeling derivative works and at one point states:

    "If NetHack is modified by someone else and passed on, we want its 
recipients to know that what they have is not what we distributed."

They obviously want it to be completely clear to a player whether they 
are playing NetHack or some modified version.  Distributing a modified 
version under the name "NetHack" makes it difficult for a non-technical 
player (who's unlikely to read the source files, where the license 
requires change notices to be placed) to tell whether they're playing 
"what [they] distributed".

So, even though your proposal doesn't seem to go against the letter of 
the license I would definitely say that it goes against the spirit.  In 
my opinion, this is an oversight in the license's terms; maybe it just 
never occurred to the license's author(s) that anyone would want to. 
Most folks who fork software projects seem to _enjoy_ coming up with new 
project names.  ;-)

